course: history
lesson: 44
title: The Cossacks, the Sich and the Hetmanate
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Below the Dnipro rapids the Cossacks kept a stronghold, the Zaporizhian Sich, where a council elected and could remove its leaders; Danylo calls it «the first democracy in Europe», but only Cossack men had a voice and Athens had assemblies much earlier, so «a remarkable elected brotherhood» is the true phrase.
explain: In 1648 Bohdan Khmelnytsky led a huge uprising and built a Cossack state, the Hetmanate; it is a hero's story with dark pages too, because the war brought terrible massacres of Jewish communities, and honest history names both.
explain: In 1654 at Pereiaslav Khmelnytsky made a military alliance with the Tsar; the teacher still had the old Soviet word «reunification», Danylo was closer to modern historians, and the teacher updated his notes: it was a treaty each side read differently, like two friends who remember the same deal in two ways.
explain: Then came «the Ruin» of civil wars, Mazepa's switch to Sweden lost at Poltava in 1709, Orlyk's constitution written in exile, and the empire ending the Sich in 1775 and the Hetmanate soon after; the Sich ended, but its memory still lives in songs, dances and the anthem.
explain: The wise lesson: one man can be «traitor» to one side and «hero» to another; look at why each side says it and what he was trying to protect.
fact: The Zaporizhian Sich below the Dnipro rapids was a Cossack stronghold where the council (rada) elected and could remove its leaders; only Cossack men had a voice, women were not allowed in, and Athens and Iceland's Althing had assemblies much earlier.
fact: A famous rude Cossack reply to Sultan Mehmed IV survives only in later copies, and scholars think it is probably a later literary creation; Repin's painting (1880–1891) made it world famous.
fact: In the early 17th century Cossacks in light boats called chaiky raided Ottoman Black Sea towns such as Sinop and even the outskirts of Istanbul, but they did not capture the city.
fact: In 1648 Hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky led a huge uprising against the Polish-Lithuanian nobility and created a Cossack state, the Hetmanate, with its own self-rule (autonomy); the war brought great suffering, including terrible massacres of Jewish communities.
fact: In 1654 at Pereiaslav, Khmelnytsky agreed to a military alliance with the Tsar of Muscovy (Moscow's state, later called Russia) against Poland; the Cossacks saw it as an alliance, Moscow later treated it as Ukraine joining Russia, and Soviet books called it «reunification».
fact: After Khmelnytsky died in 1657 came «the Ruin»: civil wars, rival hetmans backed by Poland, Moscow and the Ottomans, and Ukraine split along the Dnipro.
fact: Ivan Mazepa (hetman 1687–1709) built churches and schools and in 1708 switched to King Charles XII of Sweden against Peter I to save the Hetmanate's rights; Peter won at Poltava in 1709.
fact: In 1710 Pylyp Orlyk, Mazepa's successor in exile (living abroad, unable to go home), wrote the «Pacts and Constitutions» limiting the hetman's power; it was an early constitution but never came into force at home.
fact: Catherine II destroyed the Zaporizhian Sich in 1775; some Cossacks went to Ottoman lands (the Danube Sich), and others were later moved to the Kuban, where their descendants still live.
fact: The Russian Empire abolished the Hetmanate in the 1760s–1780s; Cossack officers became Russian nobles, and peasants were turned into serfs in 1783.
fact: Cossack images such as the hopak dance, the forelock and the bulava mace are central in Ukrainian culture, and the anthem calls Ukrainians «of Cossack kin», though most Ukrainians descend from peasants and townspeople.
exercise: Where was the Zaporizhian Sich? | Below the rapids of the Dnipro | In the middle of Kyiv, beside Saint Sophia | On the coast of Sweden | In the mountains of Crimea
exercise: Who could not vote at the Sich council? | Women, and anyone who was not a Cossack | The hetman | The otaman | Any Cossack who was older than twenty-five years of age
exercise: What made the Cossack letter to the sultan world famous? | Repin's painting | A Hollywood film from 1654 | The sultan's own diary | The Primary Chronicle
exercise: What do scholars think of the famous letter? | It is probably a later literary creation | It is proven, and the sultan cried when he read it | It was written by Shevchenko | It was carved in stone at Khotyn
exercise: What were chaiky? | Light Cossack boats | Curved Cossack sabres | Ottoman forts on the coast | Old Polish silver coins
exercise: Did the Cossacks capture Istanbul? | No, they raided its outskirts | Yes, and kept it for ten years | Yes, and made it the Sich | No, they never sailed at all
exercise: Who led the great uprising of 1648? | Bohdan Khmelnytsky | Hetman Ivan Mazepa of Baturyn | Pylyp Orlyk in exile | Hetman Petro Sahaidachny
exercise: What dark page belongs to the 1648 uprising? | Terrible massacres of Jewish communities | The burning of Saint Sophia Cathedral by the rebels | The selling of Crimea | Nothing, it had no dark pages
exercise: What did Khmelnytsky agree with the Tsar at Pereiaslav in 1654? | A military alliance against Poland | To give the city of Kyiv to the King of Sweden | To end all Cossack armies | To make the Tsar a Cossack
exercise: What is «the Ruin»? | Years of civil wars and rival hetmans after 1657 | The fall of Kyiv to the Mongol army of Batu in 1240 | The Chornobyl accident | A Cossack dance
exercise: Why did Mazepa switch to Sweden in 1708? | To save the Hetmanate's rights from Peter I | Because he wanted to be crowned king of Sweden himself | Because the Pope ordered him | Because he lost a bet
exercise: Who won the battle of Poltava in 1709? | Peter I | Charles XII | Ivan Mazepa | Pylyp Orlyk
exercise: What did Pylyp Orlyk write in 1710? | An early constitution limiting the hetman's power | The first big dictionary of the Ukrainian language | The letter to the sultan | The Ruska Pravda
exercise: Why did Orlyk's constitution never work in practice? | He wrote it in exile and never ruled at home | It was written in a lost language that nobody could read | Nobody could read in 1710 | The Sich burned it
exercise: Who destroyed the Zaporizhian Sich in 1775? | Catherine II | Tsar Peter the Great | Bohdan Khmelnytsky | Batu Khan's Mongols
exercise: Where did many Cossack families move after 1775? | To the Danube and later the Kuban | To Canada and Brazil across the ocean | To Sweden and Norway | To Rome and Venice
exercise: What happened to Cossack officers when the Hetmanate ended? | They became Russian nobles | They all moved back to the Sich | They were made Ottoman sultans | They became kings of Poland
test: Danylo says the Sich was the first democracy in Europe; teacher says «Athens was first». What's the best wording? | A remarkable elected military brotherhood where only Cossack men voted | The first democracy ever, with equal votes for all men and women alike | Not elected at all, a king ruled it | A copy of the Athenian assembly
test: Danylo's crushed cos the teacher called the sultan letter fake. What's the wise thing to say? | The painting and story are real culture; the letter's history is doubtful | It's 100% real, the sultan cried | Repin never painted it | Forget it, it is worthless
test: Danylo says Cossacks sailed to Istanbul in tiny boats. The teacher doubts it. Who's right? | Danylo: they raided its outskirts in light boats, though they did not take it | The teacher: Cossacks had no boats | Danylo: they captured Istanbul, held it for ten years and made it the new Sich | The teacher: they only sailed on lakes
test: Danylo says Khmelnytsky freed Ukraine in 1648 and that's all. What's missing? | It won autonomy, not lasting freedom, and brought massacres of Jewish communities | Nothing, the story is complete, and everyone in Ukraine was free from that day on | He lost every battle | The uprising was in 1991
test: The teacher's old book says Pereiaslav was a «reunification». What would modern historians say? | It was a military alliance that each side read differently | It was exactly a happy reunion | It was a trade deal for fish | It never happened
test: Danylo says after Khmelnytsky everything was fine. What really followed? | The Ruin: civil wars and Ukraine split along the Dnipro | Peace and wealth for a hundred years | The Mongol invasion | Independence in 1991
test: The teacher calls Mazepa a traitor and Danylo calls him a hero. What's the wise step? | Show why each side says it and what he tried to protect | Pick one and ban the other | Say he never existed | Flip a coin
test: Danylo says Ukraine wrote the first constitution in the world. What's accurate? | An early constitution, written in exile and never put into force | The first and longest-lasting constitution ever used in any country on Earth | A copy of the US one from 1787 | No such paper exists
test: What would happen to Cossack traditions if the Sich was destroyed? | The Sich ended, but songs, dances and memory lived on elsewhere | Every Cossack tradition vanished that day | Nothing, the Sich went on as before | The Cossacks became Swedes
test: Teacher: «Ukraine was Russia till 1991». Danylo: «Ukraine was always free». Fair answer? | Neither: Ukrainian lands were long ruled by empires but kept their own culture | The teacher's fully right | Danylo's fully right | Both are right at once
test: Danylo says every Ukrainian's a Cossack's descendant, it says so in the anthem. What's the fix? | The anthem speaks of spirit; most Ukrainians descend from peasants and townsfolk | True, every family tree has a hetman | The anthem says nothing about Cossacks | Cossacks had no children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the governance of the Zaporizhian Sich? | Its leaders were elected and removable by a council confined to Cossack men | It was governed by a hereditary king | All inhabitants, including women and children, enjoyed an equal vote in the council | It was governed directly from Istanbul
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the agreement concluded at Pereiaslav in sixteen hundred and fifty-four remains contested. | The parties understood its nature and obligations differently | Its text was composed in Mongolian | No agreement was concluded | It was signed solely by Sweden
exam: Ascertain the outcome of the battle of Poltava of seventeen hundred and nine. | Peter I defeated Charles XII and Hetman Mazepa | Hetman Mazepa captured Moscow and deposed the Tsar | Charles XII was crowned King of Ukraine in Kyiv | The Zaporizhian Sich was restored by the Swedes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the constitution composed by Pylyp Orlyk in seventeen hundred and ten. | It was an early document limiting the hetman's power, never put into force at home | It governed Ukraine for two centuries | It was the first constitution of the United States and was later copied in Philadelphia | It abolished the office of hetman
